394328356e-05, "top": [["\n\n", -6.294448394328356e-05], ["\n", -10.092594146728516]]}, {"t": "**", "lp": -0.1001550629734993, "top": [["**", -0.1001550629734993], ["-", -2.479339838027954]]}, {"t": "CC", "lp": -0.007314666174352169, "top": [["CC", -0.007314666174352169], ["There", -5.474283218383789]]}, {"t": "S", "lp": -2.5987963454099372e-05, "top": [["S", -2.5987963454099372e-05], [" Mode", -10.91407299041748]]}], "text": "Based on current technical documentation and driver behavior for Intel Arc GPUs (including the A770) under the Linux `xe` driver (or the newer `intel-mesa`/`i915` drivers), here is the breakdown:\n\n### 1. **CCS Mode 1 vs. Mode 2**\n\n**CCS (Constant Color Surface)** modes refer to how the GPU handles **color space conversions** and **gamma correction** during rendering, particularly when using **YUV** or **HDR** content.\n\n- **CCS Mode 1**:  \n  - **Standard sRGB / Linear workflow**.  \n  - Used for **SDR (Standard Dynamic Range)** content.  \n  - Applies standard gamma correction (typically 2.2 or sRGB curve).  \n  - **Default mode** for most desktop compositing and video playback.  \n  - **Use when**: You are displaying normal desktop content, SDR videos, or standard games.\n\n- **CCS Mode 2**:  \n  - **HDR (High Dynamic Range) workflow**.  \n  - Enables **extended color gamut** and **higher luminance** handling.  \n  - Requires the display and compositor to support HDR (e.g., Wayland with HDR support, or specific X11 setups).  \n  - **Use when**: You are playing HDR video content (e.g., 4K HDR movies) or using HDR-enabled games, and your display supports HDR output.\n\n> **Note**: The `xe` driver (Intel's open-source driver) may not fully expose CCS modes in the same way as the Windows driver. On Linux, HDR support is still limited and often requires specific compositor support (e.g., KWin, GNOME 45+ with Wayland).\n\n### 2. **When to Use CCS Mode 4?**\n\n**CCS"}